{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "The morning sun, a blinding white disc, assaulted Arthur's eyes. He blinked, the light feeling strangely…perfect. Not harsh, but flawlessly calibrated. He sat up in bed, the sheets feeling unnaturally smooth against his skin. A faint, almost imperceptible hum vibrated through the mattress. He glanced around his room, noticing details he'd never paid attention to before: the bookshelf arranged with unnerving symmetry, the posters on the wall displaying characters with impossibly vibrant colors and flawless physiques. A wave of disorientation washed over him, quickly followed by a chilling realization. The room wasn't his room. It was…too perfect. Too clean. Too *designed*. He reached out to touch the wall, and it felt…hollow. A digital shimmer ran across its surface, and for a fleeting moment, he saw code.\n\nArthur bolted upright, heart hammering against his ribs. He stumbled out of bed, his legs feeling strangely weightless. The floorboards, polished to a mirror sheen, reflected his wide, terrified eyes. He looked out the window and saw not the familiar cityscape, but a sprawling, fantastical landscape bathed in perpetual twilight. Towering, pixelated trees swayed in a gentle breeze, and creatures that looked ripped straight from a fantasy RPG roamed the hillsides. A notification popped up in his vision – a shimmering, translucent overlay that seemed to be part of the world itself. \"Welcome to Aethelgard,\" it read, \"Player Arthur. Level 1. Objective: Survive.\" He felt a cold dread creep into his soul. He wasn't just dreaming. He was *in* a video game.\n\n\n\n***\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "The", "logprob": -0.6412928104400635}, {"token": "▁morning", "logprob": -1.4735186100006104}, {"token": "▁sun", "logprob": -0.0766761526465416}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.014847755432129}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.1270788162946701}, {"token": "▁blinding", "l